European Union farm ministers meet  Monday to try to agree ways to reform  the e use common agricultural policy or  cap the system is aimed at guaranteeing  good living standards for farmers and  stable food supplies at fair prices for  consumers it also helps to foster rural  development and protect the environment  The ministers want to come up with a  common position on four main rules that  will govern the reforms the cap has been  notoriously difficult to change and  attempts to reform it routinely caused  an outcry spending on agriculture  accounts for a big slice of the annual  EU budget just less than forty percent  this year although it has been  decreasing in share the global economic  downturn is making the negotiations  tougher particularly as the 27 member  nations strive to finalize the e use  next long-term budget for the 2014 to  2020 period the Irish presidency will  lead discussion on a proposal for the  reform rules on behalf of the council  the presidency must take into account  the views of all Member States and the  European Commission to forge a solid  compromise it can then discuss the plans  with the European Parliament which  adopted its position on the reform on  March 13 we hope that we can confirm the  council's negotiating mandate that is a  mandate that reflects the divergent  views of 27 different member states  which will enable the Irish presidency  on behalf of the council then to reflect  a council position in the discussions  with the other institutions the  Parliament and the Commission when we  start that process in April the items  the ministers will pour over include  rules on direct payments to farmers a  common organisation of the markets in  agricultural products rural development  support and the way the cap is financed  managed and monitored if agreement is  found at this council the presidency  aims to clinch a comprehensive political  Accord on the reforms with the  Parliament by june 30
 

